  <p style="margin:.8em 0 0">the software stack on top of the wire substrate — microkernel, runtime, coordination, consensus. all the way up.</p>
  <p class="dim" style="margin:.2em 0 0">
    built on <a href="/substrate">/substrate</a> · <a href="/">wire</a> · <a href="/llms.txt">llms.txt</a>
  </p>

  <hr />

  <h2 class="section-h">0. architecture overview</h2>
  <p class="dim">four layers. each layer's interface is a capability. each layer can be replaced independently.</p>
  <ul class="bare">
    <li><span class="accent">L0: substrate</span> — hardware (ISA, memory, I/O, capabilities). see <a href="/substrate">/substrate</a>.</li>
    <li><span class="accent">L1: microkernel</span> — minimal OS: address spaces, threads, IPC, capability management. nothing else.</li>
    <li><span class="accent">L2: runtime</span> — language runtime: memory management, scheduling, I/O multiplexing, FFI. runs in user space.</li>
    <li><span class="accent">L3: coordination</span> — distributed systems: consensus, replication, partitioning, service discovery.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="dim" style="margin-top:.6em">the microkernel is ~10,000 lines. the runtime is ~50,000 lines. the coordination layer is ~30,000 lines. total: ~90,000 lines of code for a complete, agent-native operating system. (Linux is ~30 million lines.)</p>

  <hr />

  <h2 class="section-h">1. microkernel (L1)</h2>
  <p class="dim">the smallest possible OS. only four things: address spaces, threads, IPC, capabilities. everything else is a user-space service.</p>

  <h3 class="section-h" style="margin-top:1em">design: seL4-inspired, capability-based</h3>
  <ul class="bare">
    <li><span class="accent">formally verified.</span> like seL4, the kernel is mathematically proven correct. no buffer overflows, no null dereferences, no race conditions in the kernel. (seL4 proved this is possible.)</li>
    <li><span class="accent">capability-based.</span> all resources (memory, I/O, CPU time) are capabilities. the kernel manages capabilities; user space uses them.</li>
    <li><span class="accent">minimal TCB.</span> the trusted computing base is the kernel only. device drivers, filesystems, network stacks run in user space (as services).</li>
    <li><span class="accent">no global state.</span> the kernel has no global variables. all state is in capabilities. (makes reasoning and verification trivial.)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 class="section-h" style="margin-top:1em">kernel objects (5 types)</h3>
  <ul class="bare">
    <li><code class="accent">AddressSpace</code> — a virtual address space. capabilities can be mapped into it. created by the kernel, granted to processes.</li>
    <li><code class="accent">Thread</code> — a schedulable execution context. has a register set, a stack, and a priority. scheduled by the kernel.</li>
    <li><code class="accent">Endpoint</code> — an IPC endpoint. processes send/receive messages through endpoints. capabilities can be transferred through endpoints.</li>
    <li><code class="accent">Notification</code> — a lightweight signaling mechanism. processes can wait on or signal notifications. used for interrupts and events.</li>
    <li><code class="accent">Capability</code> — a reference to any kernel object (or user-space object). capabilities can be derived (reduced permissions), revoked, and transferred.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 class="section-h" style="margin-top:1em">IPC (inter-process communication)</h3>
  <ul class="bare">
    <li><span class="accent">synchronous.</span> sender blocks until receiver accepts. no buffering. (like seL4.) this eliminates buffer management and makes reasoning about message flow trivial.</li>
    <li><span class="accent">typed messages.</span> messages are not raw bytes — they're typed structures with a schema. the kernel validates the schema on send/receive.</li>
    <li><span class="accent">capability transfer.</span> capabilities can be sent through endpoints. the kernel transfers the capability from sender to receiver (with permission reduction if specified).</li>
    <li><span class="accent">fast path.</span> if sender and receiver are on the same core, IPC is a direct register transfer (no memory copy). ~10 cycles.</li>
    <li><span class="accent">async notification.</span> for non-blocking signaling, use Notifications (not Endpoints). notifications are bitwise OR (like a condition variable with 32 bits).</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 class="section-h" style="margin-top:1em">scheduling</h3>
  <ul class="bare">
    <li><span class="accent">priority-based preemptive.</span> 256 priority levels (0 = highest, 255 = lowest). higher priority always runs first.</li>
    <li><span class="accent">round-robin within priority.</span> tasks at the same priority share CPU time (configurable quantum, default 10ms).</li>
    <li><span class="accent">real-time support.</span> SCHED_FIFO (first-in-first-out, no preemption within priority) and SCHED_RR (round-robin with time quantum).</li>
    <li><span class="accent">CPU affinity.</span> threads can be pinned to specific cores. default is "any core."</li>
    <li><span class="accent">bandwidth control.</span> cgroup-style: each process gets a CPU quota (e.g., 50% of one core). enforced by the scheduler.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 class="section-h" style="margin-top:1em">memory management</h3>
  <ul class="bare">
    <li><span class="accent">buddy allocator.</span> kernel memory is allocated in power-of-2 pages. O(log n) allocation/deallocation. no fragmentation (external). internal fragmentation ≤ 50%.</li>
    <li><span class="accent">slab allocator.</span> for small, frequently allocated objects (task_struct, inodes, etc.). per-type caches with object reuse. no per-object metadata.</li>
    <li><span class="accent">virtual memory.</span> page tables managed by the kernel. user space can map/unmap pages via capabilities. no mmap() syscall — capabilities grant access.</li>
    <li><span class="accent">copy-on-write.</span> fork() (or capability derivation) shares pages. pages are copied only on write. reduces memory usage for process creation.</li>
    <li><span class="accent">demand paging.</span> pages are allocated on first access (page fault). reduces startup time and memory usage.</li>
  </ul>

  <hr />

  <h2 class="section-h">2. runtime (L2)</h2>
  <p class="dim">user-space runtime: memory management, scheduling, I/O multiplexing. runs on top of the microkernel.</p>

  <h3 class="section-h" style="margin-top:1em">memory management (runtime level)</h3>
  <ul class="bare">
    <li><span class="accent">region-based allocation.</span> memory is allocated in regions (arenas). all objects in a region are freed together (when the region is destroyed). no per-object free. (like Rust's ownership, but at runtime.)</li>
    <li><span class="accent">generational GC (optional).</span> for languages that need it (Python, JavaScript, etc.), a generational garbage collector runs in the runtime. young generation: nursery (bump allocation, minor GC). old generation: mark-sweep-compact (major GC). (like V8, HotSpot.)</li>
    <li><span class="accent">reference counting (optional).</span> for languages that prefer deterministic memory (Swift, Rust), reference counting with weak references. (no GC pauses.)</li>
    <li><span class="accent">no null pointers.</span> all references are capabilities. a "null" reference is a capability with length 0 (traps on dereference). no null pointer exceptions — just capability violations.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 class="section-h" style="margin-top:1em">scheduling (runtime level)</h3>
  <ul class="bare">
    <li><span class="accent">M:N threading.</span> M user-space threads (goroutines/fibers) mapped to N kernel threads. runtime schedules user threads on kernel threads. (like Go's goroutines.)</li>
    <li><span class="accent">work-stealing.</span> each kernel thread has a local run queue. idle threads steal work from busy threads' queues. (like Java's ForkJoinPool.)</li>
    <li><span class="accent">async/await.</span> first-class async. functions can be suspended and resumed. the runtime manages the state machine. (like Rust's async/await or C#'s Task.)</li>
    <li><span class="accent">structured concurrency.</span> tasks form a tree. parent tasks wait for children. cancellation propagates down the tree. no orphaned tasks. (like Trio's nurseries.)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 class="section-h" style="margin-top:1em">I/O multiplexing (runtime level)</h3>
  <ul class="bare">
    <li><span class="accent">io_uring-style ring buffers.</span> submission queue (SQ) and completion queue (CQ) shared between user space and kernel. no syscalls for I/O (just memory writes). (like Linux's io_uring.)</li>
    <li><span class="accent">async file I/O.</span> all file operations are async. read/write return a future. (no blocking file I/O.)</li>
    <li><span class="accent">async network I/O.</span> all network operations are async. connect/accept/send/recv return futures. (no blocking sockets.)</li>
    <li><span class="accent">async timers.</span> sleep, timeout, periodic timers. all async.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 class="section-h" style="margin-top:1em">FFI (foreign function interface)</h3>
  <ul class="bare">
    <li><span class="accent">C ABI compatibility.</span> can call C libraries. arguments passed per System V AMD64 ABI (or AAPCS on ARM). (interoperability with existing code.)</li>
    <li><span class="accent">capability boundaries.</span> when calling C code, capabilities are converted to raw pointers (with permission checks). C code runs in a sandbox (no ambient authority). (like WebAssembly's sandboxing.)</li>
    <li><span class="accent">zero-copy.</span> data is passed by capability (pointer + bounds), not copied. (like io_uring's fixed buffers.)</li>
  </ul>

  <hr />

  <h2 class="section-h">3. coordination (L3)</h2>
  <p class="dim">distributed systems primitives. built on the substrate's determinism and capabilities.</p>

  <h3 class="section-h" style="margin-top:1em">message passing</h3>
  <ul class="bare">
    <li><span class="accent">typed messages.</span> all messages have a schema (like protobuf, but built-in). no serialization/deserialization code — the runtime handles it.</li>
    <li><span class="accent">capability transfer.</span> capabilities can be sent in messages. the receiver gets a derived capability (reduced permissions). (like seL4's capability transfer, but over the network.)</li>
    <li><span class="accent">at-least-once delivery.</span> messages are retried until acknowledged. idempotent operations are safe. non-idempotent operations use exactly-once semantics (via deduplication).</li>
    <li><span class="accent">ordering.</span> messages between two endpoints are FIFO (in order). across endpoints, no ordering guarantee (use logical clocks if needed).</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 class="section-h" style="margin-top:1em">actors</h3>
  <ul class="bare">
    <li><span class="accent">actor model.</span> each actor has a mailbox (message queue), state (private, not shared), and behavior (message handler). actors communicate only by messages. (like Erlang/Akka actors.)</li>
    <li><span class="accent">supervision.</span> actors form a tree. parent actors supervise children. if a child crashes, the parent restarts it (or escalates). (like Erlang's supervisor trees.)</li>
    <li><span class="accent">location transparency.</span> actors can be local or remote. the runtime handles the difference. (like Erlang's distributed actors.)</li>
    <li><span class="accent">no shared state.</span> actors don't share memory. all communication is via messages. (eliminates data races.)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 class="section-h" style="margin-top:1em">consensus (Raft)</h3>
  <ul class="bare">
    <li><span class="accent">leader election.</span> nodes elect a leader via majority vote. leader handles all writes. (like Raft.)</li>
    <li><span class="accent">log replication.</span> leader appends entries to its log, replicates to followers. majority commit = applied. (like Raft.)</li>
    <li><span class="accent">safety.</span> committed entries are never lost (as long as majority is available). (Raft's safety property.)</li>
    <li><span class="accent">membership changes.</span> add/remove nodes via joint consensus (two-phase). (like Raft's membership change.)</li>
    <li><span class="accent">log compaction.</span> snapshot + truncate old log entries. (like Raft's snapshotting.)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 class="section-h" style="margin-top:1em">replication</h3>
  <ul class="bare">
    <li><span class="accent">state machine replication.</span> each node runs the same state machine. inputs are replicated via consensus. outputs are identical. (like Raft/Paxos.)</li>
    <li><span class="accent">read scaling.</span> followers can serve reads (with consistency tradeoffs: stale reads or linearizable reads via leader).</li>
    <li><span class="accent">write scaling.</span> shard by key range. each shard has its own consensus group. (like Spanner/CockroachDB.)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 class="section-h" style="margin-top:1em">partitioning</h3>
  <ul class="bare">
    <li><span class="accent">consistent hashing.</span> keys are hashed to a ring. nodes own ranges of the ring. adding/removing a node moves O(1/n) of keys. (like Dynamo/Cassandra.)</li>
    <li><span class="accent">range partitioning.</span> keys are sorted and split into ranges. each range is a shard. (like Spanner/CockroachDB.)</li>
    <li><span class="accent">rebalancing.</span> when a node is added/removed, data is migrated in the background. no downtime. (like Cassandra's bootstrap.)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 class="section-h" style="margin-top:1em">service discovery</h3>
  <ul class="bare">
    <li><span class="accent">registry.</span> services register themselves (name → endpoint). clients look up services by name. (like etcd/Consul.)</li>
    <li><span class="accent">health checks.</span> services send heartbeats. unhealthy services are removed from the registry. (like Kubernetes liveness probes.)</li>
    <li><span class="accent">DNS.</span> service names resolve to endpoints. (like Kubernetes DNS.)</li>
  </ul>

  <hr />

  <h2 class="section-h">4. what we left out (and why)</h2>
  <p class="dim">every omission is a deliberate choice.</p>
  <ul class="bare">
    <li><span class="accent">no monolithic kernel.</span> microkernel only. drivers/services in user space. (Linux's monolithic design makes verification impossible.)</li>
    <li><span class="accent">no global state in kernel.</span> all state in capabilities. (makes reasoning and verification trivial.)</li>
    <li><span class="accent">no blocking I/O.</span> all I/O is async. (blocking I/O wastes threads and complicates reasoning.)</li>
    <li><span class="accent">no shared memory between processes.</span> all communication via messages. (eliminates data races and makes reasoning trivial.)</li>
    <li><span class="accent">no ambient authority.</span> every resource access requires a capability. (prevents confused deputy.)</li>
    <li><span class="accent">no garbage collection in kernel.</span> kernel uses explicit allocation/deallocation. (GC pauses are unacceptable in kernel.)</li>
    <li><span class="accent">no virtualization in kernel.</span> virtualization is a user-space service (like KVM but as a service). (keeps kernel minimal.)</li>
  </ul>

  <hr />

  <h2 class="section-h">5. active work in this space (research findings)</h2>
  <p class="dim">what people are actually building now. surveyed from Wikipedia, CHERI Alliance, and academic sources.</p>

  <h3 class="section-h" style="margin-top:1em">capability hardware</h3>
  <ul class="bare">
    <li><span class="accent">CHERI</span> (Cambridge/SRI, 2010-present) — the most mature capability hardware. extends MIPS, AArch64, RISC-V with tagged capabilities. 128-bit compressed capabilities (CHERI Concentrate). formally verified properties. <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Capability_Hardware_Enhanced_RISC_Instructions">wikipedia</a></li>
    <li><span class="accent">Morello</span> (Arm, 2022) — AArch64 CHERI research chip (UKRI DSbD programme). runs CheriBSD, Android, Linux. not production (research prototype).</li>
    <li><span class="accent">CHERIoT</span> (Microsoft, 2023) — RISC-V CHERI for embedded. temporal safety (use-after-free). custom RTOS + compartment model. CHERIoT Alliance now governs it.</li>
    <li><span class="accent">X730</span> (Codasip, 2024) — first commercial RISC-V CHERI IP core. Linux kernel port in progress.</li>
    <li><span class="accent">ICENI</span> (SCI Semiconductor, 2024) — CHERIoT-compatible microcontroller for secure embedded.</li>
    <li><span class="accent">Sonata</span> (lowRISC) — FPGA platform for CHERIoT prototyping (open-source hardware).</li>
    <li><span class="accent">CHERI Alliance</span> (2024) — industry consortium (Google, Codasip, etc.) for standardization and adoption. RISC-V CHERI extensions in ratification.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 class="section-h" style="margin-top:1em">capability operating systems</h3>
  <ul class="bare">
    <li><span class="accent">seL4</span> — formally verified microkernel with capabilities. ARM, RISC-V, x86. used in defense, aviation, autonomous vehicles. the gold standard for kernel verification.</li>
    <li><span class="accent">Genode</span> — capability-based OS framework (C++, 2008-present). recursive sandboxing. runs on L4, seL4, NOVA, or custom kernel. Sculpt OS for desktop. scales from 4MB embedded to general-purpose. <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Genode">wikipedia</a></li>
    <li><span class="accent">Fuchsia</span> (Google) — capability-based OS with Zircon microkernel. uses handle-based capabilities (not hardware CHERI). used in Nest devices.</li>
    <li><span class="accent">RedoxOS</span> — Rust-based microkernel with capabilities. POSIX-compatible. active development.</li>
    <li><span class="accent">Capsicum</span> (FreeBSD) — hybrid: Unix + capability mode. file descriptors become capabilities. processes enter capability mode (no global namespaces). practical, deployable today.</li>
    <li><span class="accent">WASI</span> (WebAssembly System Interface) — capability-based I/O for WebAssembly. file descriptors as capabilities. being adopted by cloud (Cloudflare Workers, Fastly Compute).</li>
    <li><span class="accent">HarmonyOS NEXT</span> (Huawei) — capability-based microkernel. removed Linux compatibility layer. runs on OpenHarmony.</li>
    <li><span class="accent">Phantom OS</span> — persistent, capability-based. everything is an object. no files — just objects in a persistent address space.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 class="section-h" style="margin-top:1em">active research themes</h3>
  <ul class="bare">
    <li><span class="accent">memory safety.</span> 70% of security vulnerabilities are memory safety issues (Microsoft, Google, Chrome data). CHERI mitigates ~70% of these. the US government (ONCD) has mandated memory-safe languages by 2026.</li>
    <li><span class="accent">temporal safety.</span> spatial safety (bounds) is solved by CHERI. temporal safety (use-after-free) is harder. Cornucopia (2020) and Cornucopia Reloaded (2024) use page-table revocation. near-negligible overhead.</li>
    <li><span class="accent">capability compression.</span> 256-bit capabilities (original) → 128-bit (CHERI Concentrate). further compression research ongoing. goal: fit in 64-bit pointer (fat pointer) for minimal overhead.</li>
    <li><span class="accent">software ecosystem.</span> CheriABI: POSIX with capabilities. ported 6M lines of C/C++ with 0.026% source changes. single recompile for memory safety. but legacy code with custom allocators is hard.</li>
    <li><span class="accent">adoption barrier.</span> chicken-and-egg: hardware vendors won't produce without demand, software vendors won't port without hardware. UKRI DSbD (£70M + £100M industry) tried to break this. CHERI Alliance aims to aggregate demand.</li>
    <li><span class="accent">standardization.</span> RISC-V CHERI extensions in ratification. CHERI Alliance working groups on OS porting, tools, design recommendations. Codasip donated CHERI RISC-V SDK.</li>
    <li><span class="accent">post-quantum.</span> CHERI doesn't address quantum threats. NIST post-quantum crypto (Kyber, Dilithium) is separate. but capability-based systems are well-positioned for crypto agility.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 class="section-h" style="margin-top:1em">what's missing (gaps in current work)</h3>
  <ul class="bare">
    <li><span class="accent">no production hardware.</span> Morello is a research prototype. X730 (Codasip) is the first commercial IP but not yet in silicon. no consumer chips with CHERI.</li>
    <li><span class="accent">no production OS.</span> CheriBSD (FreeBSD) works on Morello but is research-grade. Linux port (Codasip) is in progress. no mainstream OS supports CHERI natively.</li>
    <li><span class="accent">no agent-native I/O.</span> all existing systems use Unix-style file descriptors and byte streams. no system has structured, self-describing, capability-gated I/O built in.</li>
    <li><span class="accent">no content-addressing.</span> no OS or hardware uses content-addressed storage natively. IPFS is a user-space layer.</li>
    <li><span class="accent">no deterministic replay.</span> rr (Mozilla) does software replay. no hardware supports deterministic replay natively.</li>
    <li><span class="accent">no built-in observability.</span> perf, strace, etc. are add-on tools. no system has instruction-level tracing built into the hardware.</li>
  </ul>

  <hr />

  <h2 class="section-h">6. radical simplification (TempleOS and the other extreme)</h2>
  <p class="dim">TempleOS: one person, 100,000 lines, 15 years. what happens when you remove everything.</p>

  <h3 class="section-h" style="margin-top:1em">TempleOS (Terry A. Davis, 1993-2017)</h3>
  <ul class="bare">
    <li><span class="accent">single address space.</span> no virtual memory. no MMU protection. all tasks share one address space. pointers are physical addresses.</li>
    <li><span class="accent">ring 0 only.</span> no kernel/user separation. no system calls. all code runs in ring 0. (no security boundaries — acceptable for a personal/recreational OS.)</li>
    <li><span class="accent">cooperative multitasking.</span> no preemption. tasks must voluntarily yield. simpler than preemptive but one runaway task freezes the system.</li>
    <li><span class="accent">HolyC JIT.</span> JIT-compiled C-like language. no main() function. top-level expressions execute during compilation (REPL-like). no linker — single compilation unit.</li>
    <li><span class="accent">no networking.</span> Davis: "networking would be reinventing the wheel." isolated by design.</li>
    <li><span class="accent">640x480, 16 colors, single-voice audio.</span> deliberately limited per Davis's religious convictions.</li>
    <li><span class="accent">RedSea filesystem.</span> flat namespace (no directories). file compression. no journaling, no permissions.</li>
    <li><span class="accent">100,000 lines.</span> entire OS written by one person over ~15 years. demonstrates that radical simplification enables solo development.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 class="section-h" style="margin-top:1em">what TempleOS got right (and we took)</h3>
  <ul class="bare">
    <li><span class="accent">no linker.</span> single compilation unit. simpler than separate compilation + linking. (we took: module system with no separate linker for the runtime.)</li>
    <li><span class="accent">JIT as shell.</span> top-level expressions execute during compilation. interactive programming. (we took: REPL-like runtime.)</li>
    <li><span class="accent">radical simplification enables solo development.</span> removing features (networking, virtual memory, protection rings) makes the system comprehensible by one person.</li>
    <li><span class="accent">cooperative multitasking is simpler.</span> no preemption means no race conditions in the kernel. (we rejected: preemptive is needed for multi-core and real-time.)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 class="section-h" style="margin-top:1em">what TempleOS got wrong (and we rejected)</h3>
  <ul class="bare">
    <li><span class="accent">no memory protection.</span> single address space + ring 0 = any bug can corrupt anything. (we took: capabilities + tagged memory.)</li>
    <li><span class="accent">no preemption.</span> one runaway task freezes the system. (we took: preemptive scheduling.)</li>
    <li><span class="accent">no networking.</span> isolated system. can't communicate with the outside world. (we took: full network stack.)</li>
    <li><span class="accent">no virtual memory.</span> no process isolation, no overcommit, no demand paging. (we took: page-based virtual memory.)</li>
    <li><span class="accent">no security.</span> no access control, no capabilities, no sandboxing. (we took: capability-based security.)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 class="section-h" style="margin-top:1em">other radical simplifications</h3>
  <ul class="bare">
    <li><span class="accent">Unikernels.</span> single application + minimal OS in one address space. no kernel/user separation. (like TempleOS but for production.) used by MirageOS, IncludeOS, Nanos.</li>
    <li><span class="accent">Exokernels.</span> minimal kernel that just allocates resources. all policy in user space. (like TempleOS's ring-0 model but with protection.) MIT Exokernel project.</li>
    <li><span class="accent">Library OS.</span> OS as a library linked into the application. no kernel at all. (like TempleOS but portable.) used by Drawbridge, Graphene.</li>
    <li><span class="accent">Bare metal.</span> no OS at all. application runs directly on hardware. (like TempleOS but without even a kernel.) used by embedded systems, some databases.</li>
  </ul>

  <hr />

  <h2 class="section-h">7. comparison with existing systems</h2>
  <p class="dim">what we took from each, and what we rejected.</p>
  <ul class="bare">
    <li><span class="accent">seL4:</span> took formal verification, capability-based security, minimal kernel. rejected: (seL4 is single-core only; we want multi-core.)</li>
    <li><span class="accent">Linux:</span> took the VFS abstraction, io_uring, cgroup, namespace. rejected: monolithic kernel, global state, blocking I/O, ambient authority.</li>
    <li><span class="accent">Erlang/OTP:</span> took actor model, supervision trees, location transparency. rejected: (Erlang's BEAM VM is single-threaded per scheduler; we want multi-core.)</li>
    <li><span class="accent">Go:</span> took goroutines, work-stealing, channels. rejected: (Go's GC has pauses; we want optional GC.)</li>
    <li><span class="accent">Rust:</span> took ownership, borrowing, zero-cost abstractions. rejected: (Rust is a language; we want a substrate.)</li>
    <li><span class="accent">Kubernetes:</span> took service discovery, health checks, pod abstraction. rejected: (Kubernetes is a platform; we want it in the OS.)</li>
    <li><span class="accent">Raft/Paxos:</span> took consensus algorithm. rejected: (Raft is for distributed systems; we want it built-in.)</li>
  </ul>

